Painless vision loss, Glare/halos, Opaque lens

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-009
Reason for Visit: Acute fall secondary to progressive visual decline and poor depth perception in low light.
Location: Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly resident who sustained a non-injury fall directly attributed to sudden spatial disorientation and visual failure in a dimly lit hallway. Urgent medical assessment was strictly required to rule out occult traumatic injury, stroke, or acute ocular pathology, and to implement immediate environmental and nursing safeguards to prevent a secondary, catastrophic fall before regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I couldn't see the step, everything was just a yellow blur with bright halos, and my night vision is completely gone."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing, painless, progressive decline in visual acuity. Recently noted severe glare and halos around lights, especially at night. Experiences a faded or yellowing of colors and severely reduced contrast sensitivity. Reports a myopic shift leading to a temporary improvement in near vision for reading, but distance and low-light vision are profoundly impaired. Tripped over an unseen threshold due to this visual deficit.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a stick, but visually impaired.
Social/Safety Concerns: Very high fall risk in the residential facility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/80 | HR: 72 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Alert, mildly shaken from the fall, sitting in bed.
Physical Exam: Full secondary survey reveals no signs of traumatic injury; no bony tenderness, full range of motion in all limbs. Neurological exam intact. Visual acuity is 6/36 bilaterally. Ophthalmoscopy reveals a loss of the red reflex. An opaque, brunescent (brownish-yellow) lens is clearly visible on gross examination of both eyes. Pupils are reactive; corneas are clear.
Environmental Observations: Hallway outside room has inconsistent, patchy lighting creating sharp shadows.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Medications administered by nursing staff; no new additions causing dizziness.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Advanced Bilateral Cataracts - Status: Exacerbated (Complicated by a mechanical fall)
Differentials:

Occult Hip Fracture - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain-free, full range of motion of bilateral hips, ability to straight-leg raise, and normal limb alignment.

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of flashing lights (photopsia), floaters, or a sudden peripheral curtain-like shadow.

Optic Neuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Painless presentation, bilateral nature, and absence of a relative afferent pupillary defect.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Residential fall risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented following a mechanical fall directly attributed to visual failure in low light. Urgent, direct clinical assessment was required to definitively clear the patient of occult traumatic injury and acute neurological events. Clinical evaluation confirmed the etiology of the fall was secondary to advanced brunescent cataracts causing severe contrast sensitivity loss and glare. By excluding acute medical or traumatic emergencies, the patient was saved from a painful, high-morbidity transfer to the emergency department. Immediate implementation of a nursing fall-prevention plan and lighting modification was executed to secure safety overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Ordered routine blood tests for general screening. Generated an urgent referral for an ophthalmology surgical consult.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately mitigate the fall risk, instruct the patient and nursing staff to utilize a 'touch-and-pause' method before navigating transitions (doorways, different flooring). By feeling the threshold with a mobility aid before stepping, the patient replaces failing visual feedback with reliable tactile feedback, providing immediate physical security and reinforcing safe navigation habits.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy review requested for mobility aid optimization.
DME/Safety (Optional): High-contrast tape to be applied to all thresholds in the patient's room by facility maintenance.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops delayed signs of head injury (vomiting, confusion, severe headache) or sudden inability to bear weight on either leg.

